---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}
const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find((c) => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      XML formatting re-indents a well-formed document so nested elements are readable, or removes
      insignificant whitespace when you minify. Validation here means well-formedness: matching tags
      and one root. Schema checks (XSD, DTD) are out of scope. Everything runs in your browser.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try the XML Formatter and Validator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How It Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The page parses your text with the browser DOMParser. When the document is well-formed, a
      serializer walks the tree and writes either indented tags (pretty print) or a compact form
      (minify). Therefore format xml and minify xml are the same parse with two serializers. When
      the parse fails, the status shows the first well-formedness error and, when the browser
      includes it, a line number.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="well-formed-vs-schema">
    <h2>Well-Formed Versus Schema-Valid <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Well-formed XML follows the grammar: one root, matched tags, legal names. Schema-valid XML
      also matches an XSD or DTD your system publishes. This page stops at well-formedness so it
      never needs to fetch a schema and never uploads your document.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="xml-formatter-example">
    <h2>XML Formatter Example <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      For example, paste
      <code>&lt;note&gt;&lt;to&gt;Ada&lt;/to&gt;&lt;from&gt;Grace&lt;/from&gt;&lt;body&gt;Hello&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/note&gt;</code>.
      Pretty print puts each element on its own indented line. Minify returns a single compact line.
      Change a close tag to the wrong case and the status reports malformed XML instead of guessing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="case-and-namespaces">
    <h2>Case Sensitivity and Namespaces <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      XML is case sensitive: <code>Book</code> and <code>book</code> are different. Namespaces bind a
      URI with <code>xmlns</code> or a prefix. Prefixed names stay as written. External DTDs are not
      loaded, which keeps the tool offline-friendly and private.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="when-to-use">
    <h2>When to Use This Page <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Use it when a log line, SOAP envelope or config snippet needs a readable layout, or when you
      want a quick well-formedness check before pasting into a ticket. Prefer it over upload-based
      formatters whenever the XML might contain secrets or personal data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Expecting XSD validation from a well-formedness check is the usual mix-up: this xml validator
      confirms matching tags, not your schema. Another mistake is assuming tag case does not matter.
      Formatting does not change XML meaning for element structure; it only changes layout.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="vs-upload">
    <h2>XML Formatter vs Uploading to a Web Service <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      An upload-based XML formatter vs uploading to a web service trade convenience for privacy.
      Prefer a browser-only pretty print xml and xml beautifier when the document may contain secrets.
      Minifying XML for a size-sensitive embed also stays local here.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
